return [
    // Sources: licence.source: Songs of the Four Nations, edited by Harold Boulton (Cramer, 1892), No. 26, pp. 134 to 138, printed in A-flat and written here in G; credits: words Harold Boulton (1884); tune traditional Welsh, "Ar Hyd y Nos" (printed 1784); licence: tune public domain, traditional, printed 1784; Boulton died in 1935; description: the first verse, sung softly like a hymn; the alto in thirds and sixths beneath; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'all-through-the-night' => 'All Through the Night is the Welsh lullaby "Ar Hyd y Nos", a traditional tune first printed in 1784, sung here with the English words Harold Boulton wrote in 1884. Our arrangement takes the melody and words from Songs of the Four Nations, which Boulton edited in 1892, moved from the book\'s A-flat down to G. It is the first verse for 2 to 4 singers, sung softly like a hymn. Every part sings the words together, note for note, so the chords move as one. The alto stays close under the tune, mostly in thirds and sixths, and the tenor and bass spend most of their time on a few notes.',
    // Sources: credits: words Robert Burns (1788); tune traditional Scottish; licence: tune public domain, traditional, published 1799 (no print named in licence.source); [The Conversation, "How Auld Lang Syne switched tunes en route to world domination"]; description: the first verse; bass holds the roots on "oo"; short, a good first try at holding your own line; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'auld-lang-syne' => 'Auld Lang Syne sets words Robert Burns wrote in 1788 to a traditional Scottish tune. Burns\'s words were first printed in 1796 to a slow tune; the tune sung today joined them in 1799, in George Thomson\'s collection of Scottish airs. This arrangement is the first verse for 2 to 4 singers, and it is short. The alto and tenor sing the words with the tune, word for word, the tenor coming back to F again and again. The bass holds the roots on "oo" in a few long, low notes. A good first try at holding your own line.',
    // Sources: licence.source: words checked against Hull, The Poem-Book of the Gael (1912), p. 119, "A Prayer"; credits: words Irish, translated by Mary Byrne (1905), versified by Eleanor Hull (1912); tune "Slane", from P. W. Joyce, Old Irish Folk Music and Songs (1909); first set to these words in the Irish Church Hymnal (1919); licence: tune printed in Joyce (1909) as "With my love on the road"; Byrne died 1931, Hull died 1935; [Hymnology Archive, "Be Thou My Vision"]; description: alto and tenor move gently in the middle, the bass walks the roots; a stretch song; decisions.md row 2026-11-01 (Be Thou My Vision's C4 to F5, a stretch song); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'be-thou-my-vision' => 'Be Thou My Vision is an old Irish prayer poem, put into English by Mary Byrne in 1905 and into verse by Eleanor Hull in 1912. Our words follow Hull\'s printing in The Poem-Book of the Gael (1912). The tune, Slane, is an Irish folk melody that P. W. Joyce printed in 1909 as a love song, With My Love on the Road; the two were first joined in the Irish Church Hymnal of 1919. Here it is one verse for 2 to 4 singers, all on the words. The tune runs wide, so the alto and tenor move gently in the middle while the bass walks beneath.',
    // Sources: licence.source: melody from the score in Wikipedia, "Londonderry Air"; credits: words Frederic Weatherly (1913); tune Londonderry Air, Irish traditional (published by George Petrie, 1855); licence: Weatherly died 1929; [Minerva Classics, "A Source Guide to the Music of Percy Grainger" (Irish Tune from County Derry), quoting Petrie]; [The Methodist Church in Britain, "I cannot tell why he, whom angels worship (StF 350)", notes on the tune]; description: slow, a stretch song; decisions.md row 2026-11-01 (Danny Boy's range, a stretch song); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'danny-boy' => 'Danny Boy sets Frederic Weatherly\'s words of 1913 to the Londonderry Air, an Irish tune George Petrie published in 1855. Jane Ross of Limavady, who collected it, could say only that it was very old. This is one slow verse for 2 to 4 singers. The tune saves its highest notes for the second half and runs wider than most beginners find comfortable, so the other parts stay steady: the alto moves little, the tenor sits on A for most of the song, and the bass holds the low notes on "oo".',
    // Sources: licence.source: melody and words of the chorus from an abc transcription (trillian.mit.edu, "Let Me Call You Sweetheart [G]"), moved from G to B; credits: words Beth Slater Whitson (1910); tune Leo Friedman (1910); licence: Whitson died 1930, Friedman died 1927; description: the sweetheart waltz; alto and tenor lean into warm barbershop chords beneath the long held notes; bass sings the roots; decisions.md row 2026-11-02 (its chords go round the circle of fifths, G#7 C#7 F#7 B); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'let-me-call-you-sweetheart' => 'Let Me Call You Sweetheart is a waltz from 1910, with words by Beth Slater Whitson and music by Leo Friedman. It is for 2 to 4 singers, all singing the words together, note for note. The tune settles on long held notes at the end of its lines, and beneath them the alto and tenor lean into warm barbershop chords while the bass sings beneath.',
    // Sources: credits: words Latin hymn "Adeste Fideles" (18th century), English words Frederick Oakeley (1841) as revised in Murray's Hymnal (1852); tune attributed to John Francis Wade (c. 1743); licence: Oakeley died 1880 (no print named in licence.source); [Hymnology Archive, "Adeste Fideles"]; description: alto and tenor fill the chords, the bass climbs and falls beneath; bright, festive; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'o-come-all-ye-faithful' => 'O Come, All Ye Faithful is the Latin hymn Adeste Fideles, with a tune attributed to John Francis Wade, about 1743. Wade earned his living copying music by hand, and the hymn survives in several manuscripts he signed. The English words are Frederick Oakeley\'s from 1841, as revised in Murray\'s Hymnal of 1852. This arrangement is the first verse and the refrain for 2 to 4 singers, everyone on the words. The alto keeps mostly a third under the tune, the tenor fills the chords, and the bass climbs and falls beneath. Bright and festive.',
    // Sources: licence.source: melody from the score in Wikipedia, "Ode to Joy"; credits: words Henry van Dyke (1907); tune Ludwig van Beethoven, Symphony No. 9 (1824); licence: van Dyke died 1933; [Hymnology Archive, "Joyful, Joyful, We Adore Thee"]; description: alto moves beneath it, the bass holds the roots on "oo", the tenor fills the chord; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'ode-to-joy' => 'Ode to Joy is the tune from Beethoven\'s Ninth Symphony of 1824, sung here with Henry van Dyke\'s hymn words of 1907, Joyful, Joyful, We Adore Thee. In a 1907 letter, van Dyke said he wrote them with Beethoven\'s music always in his head. The arrangement is one verse for 2 to 4 singers. The tune moves almost entirely by step, so it is quick to pick up; the alto stays close beneath it, the tenor fills the chord, and the bass holds the low notes on "oo".',
    // Sources: licence.source: melody, words and chords from the score in Wikipedia, "Red River Valley"; credits: words traditional (Canadian and American, 1870s to 1890s); tune traditional; licence: words printed by 1896; [Manitoba Historical Society, Manitoba History no. 72 (2013), "The True Story of the Song 'Red River Valley'"]; description: the gentle cowboy song; easy to learn by ear; song file (the parts' guide notes); [Wikipedia, "Red River Valley" (The Weekly Wisconsin, Milwaukee, 1887)]
    'red-river-valley' => 'Red River Valley is a traditional song, Canadian and American, from the 1870s to the 1890s. The oldest known written copy of its words is a manuscript found in Iowa, marked with the year 1879, and the song was in print by 1887, in a Milwaukee newspaper. This is one verse for 2 to 4 singers. The gentle tune is easy to learn by ear; the alto and tenor sing the words with it, the alto often a sixth below, and the bass holds the low notes on "oo", on just three notes.',
    // Sources: licence.source: melody and words from the score in Wikipedia, "Row, Row, Row Your Boat"; credits: words traditional (published 1852); tune traditional, The Franklin Square Song Collection (1881), credited to Eliphalet Oram Lyte; [Internet Archive, "Franklin Square Song Collection" (J. P. McCaskey, Harper & Brothers), "Row Your Boat (Round)"]; [The song's own chords (one chord, C, throughout); Wikipedia, "Round (music)"]; description: everyone sings the same short tune three times, entering two bars apart (tenor and bass an octave lower); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'row-row-row' => 'Row, Row, Row Your Boat is a traditional nursery song whose words were in print by 1852. The tune sung today appears in the Franklin Square Song Collection of 1881, a songbook for schools and homes, printed as a round and credited to E. O. Lyte. Everyone sings the same short tune three times, entering two bars apart, with the tenor and bass pitched lower for lower voices. Leave out the quick notes and every note of the tune belongs to one chord, so any voice fits any other. A cheerful first round.',
    // Sources: credits: words traditional Ukrainian shchedrivka (New Year song); tune traditional Ukrainian folk melody; licence: tune the four-note motif; Leontovych's 1916 setting is not used (no print named in licence.source); [America Magazine, "The story of 'Carol of the Bells,' a Christmas classic born in Ukraine"]; [The song's own words (a shchedrivka, a New Year's well-wishing song); Interlude, "Carol of the Bells by Mykola Leontovych: History and Recordings"]; description: the alto joins in thirds and sixths, the bass and tenor ring slowly underneath like bells; song file (the parts' guide notes): sections Soprano alone, Alto joins, Bass joins, All parts; words in Ukrainian
    'shchedryk' => 'Shchedryk is a traditional Ukrainian shchedrivka, a New Year well-wishing song, in which a swallow flies into a household and tells the family how rich their coming year will be. It is built on one short four-note figure that keeps repeating while the other voices build around it. Mykola Leontovych made a famous choral setting of it in 1916; ours is a new arrangement of the folk tune and does not use his. Sung in Ukrainian by 2 to 4 singers, it builds part by part: the soprano starts alone, the alto joins, then the bass and the tenor ring slowly underneath.',
    // Sources: licence.source: melody from R. R. Terry, The Shanty Book, Part I (Curwen, 1921), No. 9 (archive.org/details/shantybooksailor00terr); "Sailors' Songs", The New Dominion Monthly (Montreal, April 1876), p. 262, prints "Shanadore" with its tune (canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.8_06581_104/24); credits: words traditional American (19th century); licence.words: which print has each line; the capstan: Terry files it under "Windlass and Capstan"; description: the long, rolling melody; slow and spacious, with time to breathe; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'shenandoah' => 'Shenandoah is a traditional American river song from the 19th century, in print by 1876, when it appeared as "Shanadore" in The New Dominion Monthly. At sea it became a capstan shanty, sung by sailors turning the big upright winch that hauled in the anchor. This is one verse for 2 to 4 singers, slow and spacious, with time to breathe. The soprano sings the long, rolling tune, the alto and tenor sing the words gently beneath it, and the bass holds the low notes on "oo".',
    // Sources: credits: words Joseph Mohr (1816), English words John Freeman Young (1859); tune Franz Xaver Gruber (1818); licence: German 1816, English 1859, tune 1818 (no print named in licence.source); [Hymnology Archive, "Stille Nacht / Silent Night"]; description: the alto a third below most of the way; a stretch song; decisions.md row 2026-11-01 (Silent Night's C4 to F5, a stretch song); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'silent-night' => 'Silent Night has German words by Joseph Mohr from 1816 and a tune by Franz Xaver Gruber, written when Mohr asked him for music for two solo voices and choir, with guitar. It was first sung that way at Christmas Eve Mass in 1818. The English words are John Freeman Young\'s from 1859. This arrangement is the first verse for 2 to 4 singers. The tune runs wide, from low C to high F, so take the edges gently or move the key. The alto sings a third below most of the way, the tenor holds G and C, and the bass holds the low notes on "oo".',
    // Sources: licence.source: melody from the 1848 Shaker manuscript (Mary Hazzard's New Lebanon copy), as reproduced in Wikipedia, "Simple Gifts" (moved from C to G); credits: words and tune Joseph Brackett (1848); licence: Brackett died 1882; [American Music Preservation, "Joseph Brackett's 'Simple Gifts': Shaker simplicity in song"]; description: the Shaker dancing song; alto and tenor keep close beneath it on easy repeated notes; bass holds the roots on "oo"; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'simple-gifts' => 'Simple Gifts is a Shaker dancing song written by Joseph Brackett in 1848. Shaker manuscripts also call it Dancing Song or Quick Dance, and the words of its second half, about bowing, bending and turning, were dance directions. Our melody comes from the 1848 Shaker manuscript in Mary Hazzard\'s New Lebanon copy, moved from C to G. It is for 2 to 4 singers: the alto and tenor sing the words close beneath the tune on easy repeated notes, and the bass holds the low notes on "oo".',
    // Sources: licence.source: Theodore F. Seward, Jubilee Songs: As Sung by the Jubilee Singers of Fisk University (Biglow & Main, 1872), p. 28, in F; credits: traditional spiritual, attributed to Wallace Willis (published 1872); decisions.md row 2026-10-29 (keeps the book's spelling "hain't"; verse hummed by the alto, tenor and bass under the soprano's words); [W. E. B. Du Bois, The Souls of Black Folk (1903), "The Sorrow Songs" (Standard Ebooks edition)]; description: the refrain, the first verse and the refrain again; all joining on the last line; task brief: spirituals presented as songs of enslaved African Americans; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'steal-away' => 'Steal Away is a spiritual, one of the songs of enslaved African Americans, attributed to Wallace Willis. Our arrangement takes its melody and words from Theodore Seward\'s Jubilee Songs of 1872, the songs as sung by the Jubilee Singers of Fisk University, keeping the book\'s key of F and its spelling "hain\'t". W. E. B. Du Bois later called it the song of songs. For 2 to 4 singers, it has the refrain, the first verse and the refrain again. The refrain is in close harmony; in the verse the soprano sings the words while the others hum the chords, and everyone joins on the last line.',
    // Sources: licence.source: melody and words of the refrain from the 1903 M. Witmark and Sons sheet music, "You're the Flower of My Heart, Sweet Adeline" (Internet Archive, youreflowerofmyh00arms); credits: words Richard H. Gerard (1903); tune Harry Armstrong (1903); licence: Gerard died 1948, Armstrong died 1951; [National Park Service, "John Fitzgerald: Mayor of a Bigger, Better, Busier Boston"]; [American Heritage, "Honey Fitz"]; description: the barbershop favourite; alto and tenor slide through close chords, the bass walks the roots, a few ringing sevenths; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'sweet-adeline' => 'Sweet Adeline was published in 1903 as You\'re the Flower of My Heart, Sweet Adeline, with words by Richard H. Gerard and music by Harry Armstrong. Our melody and words of the refrain come from that 1903 sheet music, printed by M. Witmark and Sons. Boston\'s mayor John F. Fitzgerald, Honey Fitz, sang it at so many rallies that it became his theme song. This is a barbershop favourite for 2 to 4 singers, all on the words: the alto and tenor slide through close chords beneath the tune, and the bass walks beneath.',
    // Sources: licence.source: history in Wikipedia, "Swing Low, Sweet Chariot"; melody checked against the traditional John C. Campbell Folk School Song Book transcription on abcnotation.com; credits: traditional spiritual, attributed to Wallace Willis (published 1872); licence: words published 1872 (Jubilee Songs, Fisk Jubilee Singers); [Hymnology Archive, "Swing Low, Sweet Chariot"]; description: the chorus, the first verse and the chorus again; task brief: spirituals presented as songs of enslaved African Americans; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'swing-low' => 'Swing Low, Sweet Chariot is a spiritual, one of the songs of enslaved African Americans, attributed to Wallace Willis and printed in 1872 in Jubilee Songs, as sung by the Fisk Jubilee Singers. The tune uses just five notes, and it is built for call and response. For 2 to 4 singers, it has the chorus, the first verse and the chorus again. The alto and tenor sing the words with the tune, and the bass holds the low notes on "oo".',
    // Sources: licence.source: melody and words from Sharp and Marson, Folk Songs from Somerset, third series (Simpkin, 1906), no. 66, "O Waly, Waly"; credits: words as collected by Cecil Sharp and Charles Marson (1906), verses 1 and 6; tune sung by Caroline Cox of High Ham, collected by Cecil Sharp (1906); licence: Sharp died 1924, Marson 1914; [University of Toronto Libraries, Representative Poetry Online, "Waly, Waly"]; [Just Another Tune, "The Water Is Wide" (quoting Sharp's note in Folk Songs From Somerset, Third Series, 1906)]; description: two verses, slow and lyrical; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'the-water-is-wide' => 'The Water Is Wide is a traditional folk song; an older relative, Waly, Waly, was printed in Edinburgh in 1724. Cecil Sharp heard it five times in Somerset, with tunes and words varying a lot. Our melody and words come from Sharp and Charles Marson\'s Folk Songs from Somerset, third series (1906), song 66, O Waly, Waly, its tune as sung by Caroline Cox of High Ham. This arrangement is two verses for 2 to 4 singers, slow and lyrical: the alto and tenor sing the words beneath the smooth tune, and the bass holds the low notes on "oo".',
    // Sources: licence.source: melody and words read from New Jubilee Songs, 2nd ed. (1904), pages 8 and 9, transposed from E minor to F minor; licence: words published 1901 in New Jubilee Songs as Sung by the Fisk Jubilee Singers (Frederick J. Work and John Wesley Work II; died 1942 and 1925); credits: traditional spiritual (published 1901); [Song of America (Hampsong Foundation), "The Shift to the Concert Stage: 'Wade in the Water' and the American Spiritual"]; description: the chorus, two verses and the chorus between and after; the soprano calls and the others answer; the bass on "doom, ba-doom"; a stretch song; task brief: spirituals presented as songs of enslaved African Americans; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'wade-in-the-water' => 'Wade in the Water is a spiritual, one of the songs of enslaved African Americans, printed in 1901 in New Jubilee Songs as Sung by the Fisk Jubilee Singers, the book credited to Frederick J. Work and John Wesley Work II. Its arrangement there set the melody and harmony many later versions built on. Our melody and words are read from the book\'s second edition of 1904, moved from E minor up to F minor. For 2 to 4 singers, it has the chorus, two verses and the chorus between and after. In the verses the soprano calls and the others answer, while the bass keeps a steady "doom, ba-doom" underneath.',
    // Sources: credits: words John Newton (1779); tune "New Britain", American folk hymn (1829); licence: words published 1779, tune published 1829 (no print named in licence.source); [Hymnology Archive, "Amazing grace!"]; [The tune's own notes (G, A, B, D and E)]; description: a gentle first harmony; the bass holds the roots on "oo"; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'amazing-grace' => 'Amazing Grace sets John Newton\'s words, first printed in 1779 in Olney Hymns, to New Britain, an American folk hymn tune. The tune survives from 1828 on the back of a letter and was printed in 1829 in Columbian Harmony, a book whose notes have shapes that show their place in the scale. It uses only five different notes. This arrangement is the first verse for 2 to 4 singers, a gentle first harmony: the alto moves a third or a sixth beneath the tune on almost every note, the tenor holds almost every note on G, and the bass holds the low notes on "oo".',
    // Sources: credits: words traditional English version; tune "Frere Jacques", traditional French (published 1780); licence: words traditional, 19th century (no print named in licence.source); [Wikipedia, "Frère Jacques", its words]; description: everyone sings the same tune, entering two bars apart (tenor and bass an octave lower); the easiest way into singing parts; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'are-you-sleeping' => 'Are You Sleeping is the English version of Frère Jacques, a traditional French tune published in 1780; the English words are traditional, from the 19th century. In the original words, the sleeping brother is late to ring the bells for matins, the early morning prayers, and the ding, dang, dong is those bells. Here it is a round for 2 to 4 singers: everyone sings the same tune twice, entering two bars apart, with the tenor and bass pitched lower for lower voices, and the harmony happens by itself. The easiest way into singing parts.',
    // Sources: licence.source: melody and words from the score in Wikipedia, "Michael, Row the Boat Ashore"; credits: traditional spiritual (Slave Songs of the United States, 1867); [Wikipedia, "Michael, Row the Boat Ashore"]; description: a short, easy tune with a "Hallelujah" to answer; the alto stays close by, mostly on one held note; task brief: spirituals presented as songs of enslaved African Americans; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'michael-row' => 'Michael, Row the Boat Ashore is a spiritual, one of the songs of enslaved African Americans, printed in 1867 in Slave Songs of the United States. The Michael of the song is usually taken to be the archangel Michael. It is a short, easy tune with a Hallelujah to answer each line, for 2 to 4 singers: the alto stays close by, mostly on one held note, the tenor fills the chord, and the bass holds the low notes on "oo".',
    // Sources: credits: words Traditional (published 1912); tune "Good Morning to All", Mildred J. Hill (1893); licence: words public domain, the US copyright claim was ruled invalid (2015), expired in the EU (2017); tune published 1893; [Wikipedia, "Happy Birthday to You", its origins]; [Marya v. Warner/Chappell Music (US District Court, Central District of California, 2015); Wikipedia, "Happy Birthday to You", its copyright]; [Wikipedia, "Happy Birthday to You", its copyright]; decisions.md row 2026-10-27 ("dear friend" for the name); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'happy-birthday' => 'Happy Birthday takes its tune from Good Morning to All, a song to start the school day, printed in 1893 in Song Stories for the Kindergarten by the sisters Mildred and Patty Hill. The birthday words are traditional and were published in 1912. This arrangement is for 2 to 4 singers, with "dear friend" in place of a name: the alto sings mostly a third or sixth below, the tenor fills the chord, and the bass holds the low notes on "oo".',
    // Sources: licence.source: Daisy Bell, song, written and composed by Harry Dacre (T. B. Harms & Co., New York, and Francis, Day & Hunter, London, copyright 1892), pp. 3 to 5, in G, archive.org/details/daisybell00harr; credits: words and tune Harry Dacre (1892; died 1922); song facts: Tempo di Valse, the chorus marked a little faster (the same sheet music); decisions.md row 2026-11-01 (verse 1 and the chorus, our close harmony on Dacre's chords)
    'daisy-bell' => 'Daisy Bell, the song many know as A Bicycle Built for Two, was written and composed by Harry Dacre and published in 1892. Our arrangement is read from that 1892 sheet music, printed by T. B. Harms in New York and Francis, Day and Hunter in London, in its own key of G. It is a waltz, marked Tempo di Valse, and its chorus is marked to go a little faster than the verse. This is the first verse and the chorus for 2 to 4 singers, all on the words: the tune on top, the alto close beneath it, mostly on the third of each chord, the tenor filling the chord and the bass beneath.',
    // Sources: [Wikipedia, "Joy to the World": Watts, The Psalms of David Imitated (1719), Psalm 98; Mason, The Modern Psalmist (1839), "Antioch"]; licence.source: Mason and Webb, The National Psalmist (Boston, 1848), p. 132, archive.org/details/nationalpsalmist00maso; song facts (1848 tenor and bass a bar behind); decisions.md row 2026-11-07 (in D, verse 1)
    'joy-to-the-world' => 'Joy to the World sets Isaac Watts\'s words, first published in 1719 in his Psalms of David and based on Psalm 98, to the tune Antioch, which Lowell Mason printed in 1839. Our arrangement is read from Mason\'s own four parts in The National Psalmist of 1848, in D, the key of his 1839 printing. This is the first verse for 2 to 4 singers, bright and steady: the tune runs straight down the scale on "Joy to the world", the soprano and alto sing "Let ev\'ry heart prepare him room" mostly in thirds over the bass\'s held note, and on "And heav\'n and nature sing" the tenor and bass come in a bar behind the others, as choirs still sing it today.',
    // Sources: [Wikipedia, "Hark! The Herald Angels Sing": Wesley, Hymns and Sacred Poems (1739); Whitefield, Collection of Hymns for Social Worship (1753), the opening couplet; Mendelssohn 1840, Cummings]; licence.source: Hymns Ancient and Modern (ed. W. H. Monk, 1875), hymn 60; decisions.md row 2026-11-07 (in G at 92, verse 1 and the refrain)
    'hark-the-herald' => 'Hark! The Herald Angels Sing began as Charles Wesley\'s Hymn for Christmas-Day of 1739, and George Whitefield rewrote its opening lines in 1753. The tune is Felix Mendelssohn\'s, from a festival song of 1840, fitted to Wesley\'s words by William H. Cummings in the 1850s. Our arrangement is read from the four parts of Hymns Ancient and Modern, in its standard edition of 1875, in G as printed. This is the first verse and the refrain for 2 to 4 singers, all on the words, bright and festive, with a breath at every line.',
    // Sources: song facts [Wikipedia, "Jingle Bells": Pierpont, 1857, The One Horse Open Sleigh, no word of Christmas]; licence.source: Heart Songs Dear to the American People (Chapple, Boston, 1909), pp. 148 to 149, and Songs the Whole World Sings (Appleton, 1915), p. 177; decisions.md row 2026-11-07 (in G at 120, verse 1 and the chorus, "dm" and "jing")
    'jingle-bells' => 'Jingle Bells was written by James Lord Pierpont and published in 1857 as The One Horse Open Sleigh: a song about a sleigh ride, with no word of Christmas, which became a Christmas song later. Our arrangement follows the verse and chorus as printed in Heart Songs of 1909, the earliest printing we could read with today\'s chorus, set by Stackapella on that page\'s harmony, in G. This is the first verse and the chorus for 2 to 4 singers, bright and quick: the tune on top, the alto mostly a sixth under it, the tenor filling the chord, and the bass trotting on "dm" in the verse and ringing "jing" in the chorus.',
    // Sources: song facts [Bramley and Stainer, Christmas Carols New and Old (Novello, 1871), No. 1 and its index: words and tune traditional]; licence.source: the same, archive.org/details/christmascarolsn00staiiala; decisions.md row 2026-11-07 (in E minor at 120, verse 1 and the refrain, "God rest you" as printed)
    'god-rest-ye' => 'God Rest Ye Merry, Gentlemen is a traditional English carol: nobody knows who first wrote its words or its tune. It is carol number 1 in Christmas Carols New and Old of 1871, edited by H. R. Bramley and John Stainer, and our arrangement is read from Stainer\'s four parts there, in E minor as printed, with the words as the book gives them, "God rest you, merry gentlemen". This is the first verse and its refrain for 2 to 4 singers, brisk and bright: the minor key\'s sharpened D runs through the alto\'s line, and on "O tidings of comfort and joy" the harmony brightens into G major before the last "joy" comes home to E minor.',
    // Sources: song facts [Wikipedia, "Deck the Halls": Nos Galan, Edward Jones 1794; Thomas Oliphant 1862]; licence.source: The Golden Book of Favorite Songs (Hall and McCreary, 1923), p. 66, "Deck The Hall"; decisions.md row 2026-11-07 (in E-flat at 120, verse 1)
    'deck-the-halls' => 'Deck the Halls is sung to a Welsh tune, Nos Galan, which means New Year\'s Eve; the harpist Edward Jones printed it in 1794. The English words first appeared in 1862, written by the Scottish musician Thomas Oliphant for a book of Welsh melodies. Our arrangement is read from the four parts of The Golden Book of Favorite Songs, an American songbook of 1923, a whole tone lower, in E-flat. This is the first verse for 2 to 4 singers, bright and quick, everyone on the words: the alto sings most of the "fa la la"s a third under the tune, and the rising "fa la la" in the middle climbs to the song\'s top note.',
    // Sources: [Wikipedia, "Away in a Manger": in print by 1882; the Luther attribution and Murray 1887; Kirkpatrick's tune in Around the World with Christmas (1895), standard in Britain, Ireland and Canada, Mueller in the US]; licence.source: Kirkpatrick and Fillmore, Joy and Praise ([1908]), No. 174, archive.org/details/joypraisesundays00kirk; decisions.md row 2026-11-07 (in G at 84, verse 1, the tenor ours)
    'away-in-a-manger' => 'Away in a Manger is an anonymous American carol, in print by 1882. Early books credited its words to Martin Luther, but historians reject that. The tune is William J. Kirkpatrick\'s, first published in 1895 and later called Cradle Song; it is the usual tune in Britain, Ireland and Canada, while most Americans sing another, Mueller. Kirkpatrick set it for one or two voices with a light accompaniment, and our arrangement is read from Joy and Praise, a book he edited with J. H. Fillmore: the soprano and alto sing his two voices, the alto smoothed in places, the bass is his accompaniment\'s lowest note, and the tenor is ours, on his harmony. This is the first verse for 2 to 4 singers, in G, slow and gentle.',
    // Sources: credits: words Numbers 6:24 to 26 (King James Version), music Peter C. Lutkin (1900); licence.source: Lutkin's octavo (Clayton F. Summy, Chicago, 1900, plate 524-3), checked against The Methodist Hymnal (1905), No. 748; song facts [Wikipedia, "Peter Lutkin": first dean of Northwestern's School of Music, 1895 to 1928; co-edited a Methodist hymnal]; decisions.md row 2026-11-08 (in C, 4 only, no pitch changed); song file (the Amen's entries)
    'the-lord-bless-you' => 'The Lord Bless You and Keep You is Peter C. Lutkin\'s choral blessing of 1900, set to the words of Numbers 6:24 to 26 in the King James Version. Lutkin was the first dean of Northwestern University\'s School of Music. Our arrangement is read from his own printing of 1900, in C, with every pitch as he wrote it, and checked against The Methodist Hymnal of 1905, where it is No. 748. It is for 4 singers: the blessing sung together, slow and smooth, then his sevenfold Amen, where the tenor comes in first, then the bass, the alto and the soprano, rising to the soprano\'s high G.',
    // Sources: credits: words Brewster Higley (1872 or 1873), tune Daniel E. Kelley (1870s); [Wikipedia, "Home on the Range": Higley of Smith County, Kansas, wrote "My Western Home"; Kelley, a friend, set the melody; Kansas state song, June 30, 1947]; song facts [Kansas Historical Society, Kansapedia, "Home on the Range": Lomax recorded a saloon keeper in 1908 who had driven cattle up the Chisholm Trail]; licence.source: Lomax, Cowboy Songs and Other Frontier Ballads (Sturgis & Walton, 1910), pp. 39 to 40, printed in E-flat in 6/8, written here in G in 3/4; description: the verse and the refrain, as a gentle waltz; the bass on "oo", with the words only for "Home, home on the range"; decisions.md row 2026-10-29 (the source, Lomax's first edition); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'home-on-the-range' => 'Home on the Range began as My Western Home, a poem that Brewster Higley, a doctor in Smith County, Kansas, wrote in the early 1870s, and his friend Daniel E. Kelley is credited with picking out its tune on his guitar. In 1908 the folk song collector John Lomax recorded a saloon keeper singing it, a man who had once driven cattle up the Chisholm Trail. By the 1930s the song\'s origins were obscure and widely debated, until a lawsuit over it in 1934 set off a search that traced it back to Higley and Kelley, and in 1947 it became the state song of Kansas. Our arrangement is read from Lomax\'s Cowboy Songs and Other Frontier Ballads of 1910, which prints it in E-flat in 6/8; we sing it in G, as a gentle waltz. This is the verse and the refrain for 2 to 4 singers: the tune on top, the alto a third or a sixth under it on the words, the tenor filling the chord, mostly on G, and the bass holding long low notes on "oo", singing the words only for "Home, home on the range".',
    // Sources: song facts [Classical-Music.com, "Loch Lomond lyrics": earliest known printing Vocal Melodies of Scotland (1841), the tune probably older and maybe older than the words; Vaughan Williams's 1921 setting for unaccompanied choir]; song facts [The Syncopated Times, "Maxine Sullivan": the 1937 swing version made her famous]; credits: words and tune traditional Scottish; licence.source: Songs of the North, vol. I, ed. A. C. Macleod and Harold Boulton (Cramer; first published 1885), pp. 10 to 12, the melody checked against Helen Hopekirk, Seventy Scottish Songs (Ditson, 1905), printed in F and written here in G; sections: Verse 1, Chorus, Verse 2, Chorus; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'loch-lomond' => 'Loch Lomond is a traditional Scottish song. It was first printed in Edinburgh in about 1841, but it is probably older than that, and its tune may be older than its words. Ralph Vaughan Williams set it for unaccompanied choir in 1921, and in 1937 Maxine Sullivan\'s swing version made her famous. Our arrangement is read from Songs of the North, edited by A. C. Macleod and Harold Boulton and first published in the 1880s, its melody checked against Helen Hopekirk\'s Seventy Scottish Songs of 1905, and moved from the book\'s F up to G. This is two verses, each with the chorus "O ye\'ll tak\' the high road", for 2 to 4 singers, everyone on the words: the alto a third or a sixth under the tune, the tenor filling the chord, mostly on G, and the bass on a low G for more than half the song, like a drone, moving to C and D where the chords change.',
    // Sources: credits: tune Johannes Brahms, Op. 49 No. 4 (1868); words Arthur Westbrook (1901), from the German; licence: "Arthur Westbrook" a pen name of the Ditson editor Charles Fonteyn Manney; song facts [Wikipedia, "Brahms' Lullaby": a gift for Bertha Faber on the birth of her second son; first sung in public in Vienna on 22 December 1869, Clara Schumann at the piano]; licence.source: the first edition (N. Simrock, Berlin, 1868), p. 11, in E-flat; the English words from Forty Songs, ed. James Huneker (Ditson, 1903), which also checked the notes; both verses, as Brahms's repeat sings them; licence.arrangement: the tenor and bass on "oo" from his left hand's chords; description: slow and gentle, the alto under the tune on the same words; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'brahms-lullaby' => 'Brahms\'s Lullaby is the Wiegenlied, Op. 49 No. 4, that Johannes Brahms published in 1868, written as a gift for his friend Bertha Faber on the birth of her second son. Its first known public performance was in Vienna on 22 December 1869, with Clara Schumann at the piano. Our arrangement is read from the first edition, printed by Simrock in Berlin in 1868, in Brahms\'s own key of E-flat, with the familiar English words "Lullaby and good night" by Arthur Westbrook, as printed in 1903 in a Ditson book of Brahms\'s songs that also checked the notes. This is both verses, one for each time through Brahms\'s repeat, for 2 to 4 singers, slow and gentle: the tune on top, the alto under it on the words in thirds and sixths, and the tenor and bass singing soft "oo" chords taken from Brahms\'s own piano part.',
    // Sources: song facts [Thomas Ravenscroft, Pammelia (1609), No. 85, read from the facsimile: the first English book of rounds and catches, a round for five voices; the 1609 words end "fill the pot, Eadie", "yet will I be merry" much later]; credits: words and tune anonymous (printed 1609); licence.source: the facsimile at pbm.com/~lindahl/ravenscroft/pammelia/pam_44.gif, a canon at the unison for five voices, written here in F minor (a minor third higher) for up to four voices entering a bar apart; description: tenor and bass an octave lower, the harmony happens by itself, the words in today's spelling; decisions.md row 2026-11-01 (each singing it three times); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'hey-ho-nobody-home' => 'Hey, Ho, Nobody Home is a round printed in 1609 in Pammelia, the first English book of rounds and catches, compiled by Thomas Ravenscroft; nobody knows who wrote its words or its tune. There it is a round for five voices, and its words end "fill the pot, Eadie": a call to fill the drinking pot. The ending many people know today, "yet will I be merry", came later. Our round is read from a facsimile of the 1609 page, with its words in today\'s spelling, written in F minor, a minor third above the print. Here it is for 2 to 4 singers: everyone sings the same short tune three times, entering a bar apart, with the tenor and bass pitched an octave lower for lower voices, and the harmony happens by itself.',
    // Sources: credits: words Horatio G. Spafford (1873); tune Philip P. Bliss, "Ville du Havre" (1876); [Wikipedia, "It Is Well with My Soul": his four daughters, Annie, Maggie, Bessie and Tanetta, killed when the Ville du Havre sank crossing the Atlantic; he wrote the words as his ship passed near where they had died; Bliss named the tune after the ship]; song facts [Gospel Hymns No. 2 (1876), No. 76, read from the scan: the songbook Bliss and Sankey made for their revival meetings; Bliss's own four parts; the echo in the chorus written into the parts]; licence.source: the same page, in Db and written here in C; licence.arrangement: the alto and tenor revoiced so 2 and 3 singers keep each chord's third; description: the first verse and the chorus, all four sing the last line together, steady and warm; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'it-is-well' => 'It Is Well with My Soul has words by Horatio G. Spafford, written in 1873 after his four daughters were killed when the ship Ville du Havre sank crossing the Atlantic; he wrote them as his own ship passed near the place where they had died. Philip P. Bliss named his tune Ville du Havre, after the ship. The hymn was first printed in 1876, as No. 76 in Gospel Hymns No. 2, the songbook Bliss and Ira D. Sankey made for their revival meetings, and our arrangement is read from Bliss\'s own four parts on that page, moved from its D-flat down to C, with the alto and tenor revoiced so that 2 and 3 singers keep each chord\'s third. This is the first verse and the chorus for 2 to 4 singers, steady and warm, all on the words: the alto mostly a sixth under the tune, the tenor mostly on G, and the bass on the low notes of each chord. In the chorus the tune holds "well" and "soul" while the other parts answer beneath it, an echo Bliss wrote into the parts himself, and then all four sing the last line together.',
    // Sources: credits: words Reginald Heber (1826); tune John Bacchus Dykes, "Nicaea" (1861); [Wikipedia, "Holy, Holy, Holy! Lord God Almighty": written while Heber was vicar of Hodnet, Shropshire; first published posthumously in 1826; intended for Trinity Sunday; Nicaea composed for the first edition of Hymns Ancient and Modern (1861); it begins with an ascending major triad]; song facts [Hymns Ancient and Modern (Novello, 1861), Hymn 135, read from the scan: the hymn for Trinity Sunday; the index marks Nicaea as a new tune; Dykes's own four parts]; licence.source: the same, in E, printed in minims and written here in crotchets, the Amen left out; licence.arrangement: the alto and tenor revoiced so the duet and trio are complete; description: the first verse, steady and broad, every line one breath; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'holy-holy-holy' => 'Holy, Holy, Holy is Reginald Heber\'s hymn for Trinity Sunday, written while he was vicar of Hodnet in Shropshire and first published in 1826, after his death. Its tune, Nicaea, is John Bacchus Dykes\'s, written for the first edition of Hymns Ancient and Modern in 1861, where the hymn is number 135 and the index marks the tune as new; Dykes harmonised it too. Our arrangement is read from his own four parts on that page, in E as printed, with the alto and tenor revoiced so that 2 and 3 singers have whole chords, and the Amen left out. This is the first verse for 2 to 4 singers, steady and broad, every line one breath: the tune opens by climbing the notes of the home chord on "Holy, holy, holy", the alto sings mostly a third beneath it, the tenor fills the chord, and the bass sings Dykes\'s own bass line, mostly on the roots.',
    // Sources: [Wikipedia, "Go Down Moses": an African-American spiritual describing the Exodus (Exodus 5:1, "Let my people go"); enslaved African Americans related their experiences under slavery to the Israelites' under Egyptian slavery]; song facts [Seward, Jubilee Songs (Biglow & Main, 1872), pp. 22 to 23, read from the scan: twenty-four verses, from Israel in Egypt to the Red Sea and on to freedom; only the answer, "Let my people go", in four-part harmony]; credits: traditional spiritual, as sung by the Fisk Jubilee Singers (published 1872); licence.source: the same book, p. 22, in F minor and written here a tone up in G minor; verses 1 and 2, each with the refrain; decisions.md row 2026-11-02 (words as printed, "Tell ole Pha-roh"; the soprano sings every call alone); description: call and answer, slow and steady, in a minor key; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'go-down-moses' => 'Go Down, Moses is an African-American spiritual that tells the story of the Exodus, in which God sends Moses to tell Pharaoh, "Let my people go". Enslaved African Americans heard in it their own bondage, like the Israelites\' in Egypt. The Fisk Jubilee Singers\' book of 1872, Theodore F. Seward\'s Jubilee Songs, gives it twenty-four verses, from Israel in Egypt to the Red Sea and on to freedom, and writes only the answer, "Let my people go", in four-part harmony; the rest is the tune alone, each verse\'s call for one voice and "Go down, Moses" doubled in octaves. Our arrangement is read from that page, a tone higher, in G minor, with its words as printed, "Tell ole Pharoh". For 2 to 4 singers, it is two verses, each with the refrain, slow and steady, in call and answer: the soprano sings every call alone, and everyone answers "Let my people go" in harmony, the alto and tenor on two notes each and the bass on D and a low G.',
    // Sources: credits: words Ada Blenkhorn (1899), tune J. Howard Entwisle (1899); song facts [Hymnary.org, "There's a dark and a troubled side of life": Bright Melodies (1899), a Philadelphia songbook by John R. Sweney and Entwisle, who wrote its music]; song facts [Smithsonian Folkways, liner notes by Hal Bruno (FTS 32431): the Carter Family recorded it in Camden, New Jersey, in 1928, and it became their familiar theme song]; licence.source: Heavenly Sunlight (MacCalla & Co., Philadelphia, 1900), No. 40, "Keep on the Sunny Side of Life", in A-flat; decisions.md rows 2026-10-29 (Entwisle's parts kept except where the alto takes the third and the tenor the alto's note) and 2026-11-01 (only clearly hard spots in Entwisle's parts changed); description: the first verse and the chorus, bright and bouncing, the other parts on the words with the tune; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'keep-on-the-sunny-side' => 'Keep on the Sunny Side has words by Ada Blenkhorn and music by J. Howard Entwisle, and it appeared in 1899 in Bright Melodies, a Philadelphia songbook by John R. Sweney and Entwisle. The Carter Family recorded it in Camden, New Jersey, in 1928, and it became their familiar theme song. Our arrangement is read from Entwisle\'s own four parts in Heavenly Sunlight, a songbook of 1900, where it is called Keep on the Sunny Side of Life, in A-flat as printed, kept close to how he wrote them, with the alto taking the chord\'s third from the tenor in a few places. This is the first verse and the chorus for 2 to 4 singers, bright and bouncing, everyone on the words: the alto a sixth or a third under the tune, the tenor filling the chord, mostly on A-flat and E-flat, and the bass on the roots, mostly A-flat and E-flat.',
    // Sources: song facts [The Traditional Ballad Index, "Down by the Old Mill Stream", Notes: third most popular song in America in 1911; Spaeth, A History of Popular Music in America (1948), p. 372, the comic gestures]; song facts [IMSLP, "Down by the Old Mill Stream (Taylor, Tell)": the 1910 sheet music's men's-voices setting of the chorus]; credits: words and tune Tell Taylor (1910); licence.source: the first edition (Tell Taylor, Chicago, 1910), chorus "Valse lento", printed in G, written here in A at men's pitch, archive.org/details/sm_downbytheoldmillstream; decisions.md rows 2026-11-01 (true barbershop; Hillel: "Only Old Mill Stream" changes key); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'down-by-the-old-mill-stream' => 'Down by the Old Mill Stream is a waltz song by Tell Taylor, published in Chicago in 1910, and a later chart history ranks it the third most popular song in America in 1911. Its first sheet music already came with a setting of the chorus for men\'s voices, and by 1948 the music historian Sigmund Spaeth noted that audiences were being taught to sing it with comic gestures. Our arrangement is read from that 1910 first edition, voiced for barbershop and moved from G up to A. This is the chorus, for 2 to 4 singers: the lead sings the tune, the tenor rings above it, the baritone fills the chord and the bass holds the roots.',
    // Sources: song facts [Mainly Norfolk, "Blow the Man Down": a halyard shanty, the crew resting on the rope through the shantyman's line, pulling on the refrain]; song facts [The Traditional Ballad Index, "Blow the Man Down": earliest trace July 1867, the Syracuse Daily Courier; Hugill's six versions]; credits: words and tune traditional; licence.source: W. B. Whall, Ships, Sea Songs and Shanties (3rd ed., James Brown & Son, Glasgow, 1913), p. 92, printed in D in 3/8, written here in F in 3/4, archive.org/details/shipsseasongssha00whal; decisions.md row (Whall's "Way-ay", the calls solo); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'blow-the-man-down' => 'Blow the Man Down is a halyard shanty, sung while hoisting heavy yards: the crew rested on the rope while the shantyman sang his line, then took a big pull together on the refrain. Its earliest known trace is from July 1867, in a Syracuse newspaper, and the shanty collector Stan Hugill later sorted it into six separate versions. Our arrangement is read from W. B. Whall\'s Ships, Sea Songs and Shanties (third edition, 1913), keeping his "Way-ay", and moved from D up to F. This is three verses for 2 to 4 singers, brisk and swinging: the soprano is the shantyman and sings every call alone, and everyone hauls in on the answers in harmony.',
    // Sources: song facts [Cecil J. Sharp, English Folk-Chanteys (1914), No. XXVII and its note, read from the scan: John Short of Watchet; the dorian mode where other books print major]; credits: words and tune traditional (as sung by John Short, Watchet; printed 1914); licence.source: Sharp, English Folk-Chanteys (London and Taunton, 1914), No. XXVII, p. 32, in C dorian, written here in C# dorian, three of his verses, archive.org/details/englishfolkchant00shar; decisions.md row 2026-11-02 (chorus, three verses, chorus; the soprano the shantyman); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'haul-away-joe' => 'Haul Away, Joe is a pulling shanty: the shantyman sings a line alone, and everyone answers "Way, haul away, haul away, Joe", landing together on "Joe". Our arrangement is read from Cecil Sharp\'s English Folk-Chanteys of 1914: the tune John Short, an old sailor from the harbour town of Watchet, sang for it, with the harmony of Sharp\'s piano part. Other books print the shanty in a major key, but Sharp notes that Short\'s tune is in the dorian mode, which gives it a darker, older sound, and we keep it, a semitone up. This is the chorus, three verses and the chorus again, for 2 to 4 singers, the soprano singing each call alone.',
    // Sources: song facts [Sōkyokushū (Ministry of Education, Tokyo, 1888), No. 2, read from the scan: the first printing, for the koto, the tune in Western notes; the five-note in scale and its open ending]; credits: words and tune traditional Japanese (printed 1888); licence.source: Sōkyokushū, compiled by the Ongaku Torishirabe Gakari (Tokyo, 1888), No. 2, the in scale on E, written here a minor third higher, dl.ndl.go.jp/pid/857651; decisions.md rows 2026-11-01 (sung in Japanese, romaji in the lane; held notes on "oo", no Western chords) and 2026-11-08 (the held parts breathe apart, Hillel); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'sakura-sakura' => 'Sakura Sakura, the Japanese song of cherry blossom, was first printed in 1888 by Tokyo\'s music school, in a collection of music for the koto, a long zither, with the tune written in Western notes. It uses only five notes, the in scale of Japanese koto music, and ends on a note that sounds open rather than finished. Our arrangement is read from that 1888 printing, the Sōkyokushū, and moved a minor third up to sit in a beginner\'s range. It is sung in Japanese, with the words shown in romaji, slow and calm, for 2 to 4 singers: the tune over soft held notes on "oo", like a koto\'s open strings, with no Western chords.',
    // Sources: song facts [La Paloma (Century Music Publishing, 1906), read from the scan: "canción americana", the words beginning as a sailor leaves Havana; the piano's habanera rhythm throughout]; credits: words and tune Sebastián Iradier (about 1863); licence.source: La Paloma, canción americana (Century Music Publishing Co., New York, 1906, plate 84-4), pp. 2 to 5, in C, checked against Artists' Vocal Album (White, Smith & Co., Boston, 1887), pp. 66 to 69, archive.org/details/lapalomadovecanc00yrad; decisions.md row 2026-11-02 (verse 1 and the refrain in Spanish; the bass's habanera on "dm"); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'la-paloma' => 'La Paloma is Sebastián Iradier\'s habanera of about 1863, which he called a "canción americana", an American song, after the habaneras he heard in Cuba. Its words begin as a sailor leaves Havana. Our arrangement is read from a 1906 New York edition, checked against a Boston printing of 1887, in C as printed. This is the first verse and the refrain, sung in Spanish, for 2 to 4 singers. Under the tune the piano of the print plays the habanera rhythm all the way through, a long note, a short one, then two even ones, and here the bass sings it on "dm", while the alto and tenor sing the words.',
    // Sources: song facts [Mozart's Werke, Serie 3, No. 31 (Breitkopf & Härtel, 1880), read from the scan: 1791, the last year of his life, K. 618; Adagio and sotto voce]; credits: words a 13th-century Eucharistic hymn, music Mozart (1791); licence.source: Mozart's Werke, Serie 3, No. 31, K. 618, pp. 123 to 124 (Leipzig, 1880), in D, the voices only, IMSLP #78492; decisions.md row 2026-11-01 (4 singers only; the strings' interlude a bar's rest); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'ave-verum-corpus' => 'Ave verum corpus is a short motet Mozart wrote in 1791, the last year of his life, catalogued as K. 618, setting a medieval Latin hymn. He marked it Adagio and sotto voce, "under the voice": slow, and soft all the way through, without one loud note. Our arrangement is read from the 1880 Breitkopf & Härtel edition of Mozart\'s works, in D as he wrote it, with his own four voice parts and the Latin words. The strings\' introduction and ending are left out, and their short interlude becomes a bar\'s rest. This is for 4 singers only, as the chords need all four of Mozart\'s voices.',
    // Sources: song facts [Morley, A Plaine and Easie Introduction to Practicall Musicke (1597), excerpt on a University of Mississippi course page: balletts, or fa las, for dancing as well as singing]; song facts [IMSLP, "Balletts to Five Voyces, Book 1 (Morley, Thomas)": two 1595 editions from Thomas Este, English and Italian]; credits: words anonymous, after Orazio Vecchi (1595), music Thomas Morley (1595); licence.source: E. H. Fellowes, The English Madrigal School, vol. IV (Stainer & Bell, 1913), No. 3, checked against the 1595 part-books; decisions.md row 2026-11-01 (Hillel: "4 singers only"; the two tenors joined); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'now-is-the-month-of-maying' => 'Now Is the Month of Maying is a ballett by Thomas Morley, printed in London in 1595, with words after the Italian of Orazio Vecchi. In his music textbook of 1597, Morley said balletts, also called fa las, were songs for dancing as well as singing. His 1595 book of them came out in two editions, one in English and one with Italian words. Our arrangement is read from E. H. Fellowes\'s 1913 edition, its accidentals checked against the 1595 part-books, with Morley\'s two tenor parts joined into one. This is verse 1 and its "fa la" refrains, each half sung twice, for 4 singers, light and dancing, every voice with a tune of its own.',
    // Sources: song facts ["Asleep in the Deep" (New York: F. A. Mills), Lester S. Levy Sheet Music Collection, Johns Hopkins University, Box 145, Item 046, read from the scan: written for a baritone or bass voice; the last line sung twice, its last "beware!" an octave below the first]; credits: words Arthur J. Lamb, music H. W. Petrie (1897); licence.source: the F. A. Mills printing, in F, checked against M. Witmark & Sons' edition in D (IMSLP 191240), verse 1 and the refrain; decisions.md row 2026-11-02 (the bass sings the tune; the others "oo" on Petrie's harmony); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'asleep-in-the-deep' => 'Asleep in the Deep is a sea song of 1897, with words by Arthur J. Lamb and music by H. W. Petrie, who wrote it for a baritone or bass voice. That is why here the bass, not the soprano, sings the tune. Its last line keeps its lowest notes for the end: it is sung twice, the second time sinking through "asleep in the deep" to a last "beware!" a whole octave below the first. Our arrangement is read from a New York printing by F. A. Mills, checked against Witmark\'s edition. This is the verse and the refrain for 2 to 4 singers, rolling and dramatic, the other parts holding soft chords on "oo" on Petrie\'s harmony.',
    // Sources: song facts [The Pirates of Penzance, vocal score (Chappell), Act II No. 12, read from the scan: Allegro marziale, louder as it goes; two choruses at once, pirates and police]; credits: words W. S. Gilbert, music Arthur Sullivan (1879); licence.source: the Chappell vocal score (plate 23731), Act II No. 12, pp. 151 to 152, in D for men's double chorus, written here in A, archive.org/details/vocalscoreofpira00sull; decisions.md row 2026-11-02 (the women take the pirates, the men the police); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'with-cat-like-tread' => 'With Cat-like Tread is the pirates\' stealthy march from Gilbert and Sullivan\'s The Pirates of Penzance of 1879. The pirates sing about creeping in silence, but Sullivan marks their chorus Allegro marziale, like a march, and it gets louder as it goes: the joke is in the music. Our arrangement is read from Chappell\'s vocal score, the end of the Act II reprise, where two choruses sing at once, the pirates creeping along while the police answer "Tarantara!". Sullivan wrote it for men. Here, as mixed choirs sing it, the soprano and alto take the pirates and the tenor and bass the police, moved from D to A.',
    // Sources: song facts [Wikipedia, "Abide with Me": Lyte wrote it in 1847, dying of tuberculosis, and died in Nice that November; the FA Cup Final tradition, formally from 1927]; credits: words Henry Francis Lyte (1847), tune William Henry Monk, "Eventide" (1861); licence.source: Hymns Ancient and Modern (Novello, 1861), Hymn 14, in E-flat, checked against the 1875 edition, Hymn 27, archive.org/details/hymnsancientmode00unse; decisions.md row 2026-11-08 (verse 1, every line one breath; Monk's chromatic alto kept); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'abide-with-me' => 'Abide with Me is Henry Francis Lyte\'s evening hymn, which he wrote in 1847 while he was dying of tuberculosis; he died in Nice that November. Its tune, Eventide, is by William Henry Monk, who printed it in Hymns Ancient and Modern in 1861. Its first and last verses are sung before the FA Cup Final in England, a tradition that formally began in 1927. Our arrangement is read from that 1861 hymnal, in Monk\'s own four parts in E-flat, checked against the 1875 edition. This is the first verse for 2 to 4 singers, slow and warm, every line one breath.',
    // Sources: song facts [Wikipedia, "Shall We Gather at the River?": first called Beautiful River; the tune Hanson Place, after the Brooklyn church; John Ford's westerns, Stagecoach and The Searchers]; credits: words and music Robert Lowry (1864); licence.source: Lowry, Bright Jewels for the Sunday School (Biglow & Main, 1869), p. 110, "Beautiful River", in E-flat, archive.org/details/brightjewelsfors00lowr_0; decisions.md row 2026-11-08 (verse 1 and the chorus; the tenor and bass high in the chorus); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'shall-we-gather' => 'Shall We Gather at the River is Robert Lowry\'s hymn of 1864, words and music both his. He first called it Beautiful River, and its tune was later named Hanson Place, after the Brooklyn church where he sometimes served as a Baptist minister. It was one of the film director John Ford\'s favourite hymns, heard in many of his westerns, Stagecoach and The Searchers among them. Our arrangement is read from Lowry\'s own four parts in his Bright Jewels for the Sunday School of 1869, in E-flat as printed. This is the first verse and the chorus for 2 to 4 singers, cheerful and steady.',
    // Sources: song facts [Wikipedia, "Come Thou Fount of Every Blessing": Robinson 22, 1757 or 1758; Nettleton first in Wyeth's Repository, Part Second (1813), named after Asahel Nettleton, its writer not known for sure]; credits: words Robert Robinson (1758), tune "Nettleton" (1813); licence.source: The Methodist Hymnal (Eaton & Mains, 1905), No. 19, "Nettleton", in E-flat, archive.org/details/methodisthymnalo00meth; decisions.md row 2026-11-08 (verse 1, a breath at every line); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'come-thou-fount' => 'Come, Thou Fount of Every Blessing has words by Robert Robinson, who wrote them when he was 22, in 1757 or 1758 (sources differ). Its tune, Nettleton, first appears in John Wyeth\'s Repository of Sacred Music, Part Second, in 1813. It is named after the preacher Asahel Nettleton, and some credit him with it, but who wrote it is not known for sure. Our arrangement is read from the four parts The Methodist Hymnal printed in 1905, in E-flat. This is the first verse for 2 to 4 singers, bright and lilting in three, every line one breath.',
    // Sources: song facts [Wikipedia, "Nearer, My God, to Thee": Adams, 1841, Loughton, Essex; Jacob's dream, Genesis 28; the Titanic accounts disputed]; credits: words Sarah Flower Adams (1841), tune Lowell Mason, "Bethany" (1856); licence.source: The Sabbath Hymn and Tune Book, ed. Mason, Park and Phelps (Mason Brothers, 1859), p. 244, "Bethany", in G, archive.org/details/sabbtune00maso; decisions.md row 2026-11-08 (verse 1, every line one breath); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'nearer-my-god' => 'Nearer, My God, to Thee has words by Sarah Flower Adams, who wrote them in 1841 at her home in Loughton, Essex, retelling Jacob\'s dream in Genesis 28, of a ladder between earth and heaven. Some Titanic survivors said the ship\'s band played it as she sank; others denied it, so no one knows for sure. Its tune, Bethany, is by Lowell Mason, and our arrangement is read from his own four parts in The Sabbath Hymn and Tune Book of 1859, in G as printed. This is the first verse for 2 to 4 singers, calm and even, every line one breath.',
    // Sources: song facts [Wikipedia, "Greensleeves": The Merry Wives of Windsor, Falstaff's line; Christmas words since at least 1686, Dix's "What Child Is This?" (1865)]; credits: words and tune traditional English, 16th century; licence.source: William Chappell, Popular Music of the Olden Time, vol. 1 (Cramer, Beale & Chappell, 1855 to 1859), p. 230, with G. A. Macfarren's harmony, in E minor, written here a whole tone higher, archive.org/details/popularmusicofol01chapuoft; decisions.md row 2026-11-08 (verse 1 and the refrain; Macfarren's bass, our alto and tenor); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'greensleeves' => 'Greensleeves is an English tune of the 1500s, sung here in its own old words, "Alas, my love, you do me wrong". Shakespeare names it twice in The Merry Wives of Windsor, where Falstaff cries, "Let the sky rain potatoes! Let it thunder to the tune of Greensleeves!" People have sung Christmas words to it since at least 1686, the best known "What Child Is This?" of 1865. Our arrangement is read from William Chappell\'s Popular Music of the Olden Time of the 1850s, on the harmony G. A. Macfarren wrote for it, a whole tone higher. This is the first verse and the refrain for 2 to 4 singers, gentle and lilting.',
    // Sources: song facts [Wikipedia, "Jerusalem (hymn)": Parry wrote it on 10 March 1916 at Robert Bridges's request for a Fight for Right meeting; the women voters, 1917]; credits: words William Blake (c. 1804), from the preface to Milton, tune Hubert Parry (1916); licence.source: Parry's unison song (J. Curwen & Sons, 1916), read from Songs of Praise (OUP, 1925), No. 204, checked against Curwen's plate in The Old Country (Dent, 1917), in D; decisions.md row 2026-11-08 (Parry's tune and left hand, our alto and tenor); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'jerusalem' => 'Jerusalem sets William Blake\'s "And did those feet in ancient time", from the preface to his Milton of about 1804, to music Hubert Parry wrote on 10 March 1916, when the poet Robert Bridges asked him for a song for a Fight for Right meeting in London. Campaigners for women\'s votes took it up in 1917, and Parry wrote that he wished it might become the Women Voters\' hymn. He wrote it as a unison song with accompaniment: here the tune is his, the bass follows his left hand, and the alto and tenor are ours on his harmony. This is the first verse, broad and steady, in D, for 2 to 4 singers.',
    // Sources: song facts [Wikipedia, "Lift Every Voice and Sing": written for Lincoln's birthday, first recited by 500 students in 1900; the NAACP's "Negro national anthem" by 1919]; credits: words James Weldon Johnson, music J. Rosamond Johnson (1900); licence.source: Rosamond Johnson's own setting (Jos. W. Stern & Co., New York, 1900, plate 3071-3), a semitone lower; decisions.md row 2026-11-08 (his four parts smoothed for beginners; the lower parts on his organ's chords where every voice sings the tune); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'lift-every-voice' => 'Lift Every Voice and Sing has words by James Weldon Johnson and music by his brother, J. Rosamond Johnson. James wrote the poem for Abraham Lincoln\'s birthday, and 500 students first recited it in 1900. By 1919 the NAACP was calling it the "Negro national anthem". Our arrangement is read from Rosamond Johnson\'s own four voice parts in the first printing, by Jos. W. Stern of New York in 1900, a semitone lower and smoothed for beginners. Where he has every voice sing "Sing a song full of the faith" in octaves, the lower parts hold his organ\'s chords under the tune. This is the first verse for 2 to 4 singers, broad and stately.',
    // Sources: song facts [W. B. Whall, Ships, Sea Songs and Shanties (3rd ed., 1913), p. 107: sung by the whole crew at a quick march, walking away with the rope; dropped when crews got smaller]; song facts [R. R. Terry, The Shanty Book, Part I (1921), introduction: one of the shanties in the old modes]; credits: words and tune traditional (as printed by Terry, 1921); licence.source: Terry, The Shanty Book, Part I (J. Curwen & Sons, 1921), No. 14, pp. 30 and 31, in D dorian, written here a whole tone higher, archive.org/details/shantybooksailor00terr; decisions.md row 2026-11-08 (the soprano the shantyman; the chorus on Terry's harmony); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'drunken-sailor' => 'Drunken Sailor is the shanty everyone knows. The sea captain W. B. Whall wrote that the whole crew sang it together at a quick march, walking away with the rope, and that it dropped out of use when crews got too small to walk away with anything. Richard Terry, who printed this tune in 1921, named it as one of the shanties in the old modes, not a modern major or minor key. Our arrangement is read from Terry\'s book, on the harmony of his piano part, a whole tone higher. This is the verse and the chorus for 2 to 4 singers: the soprano sings the verse alone, and everyone comes in on "Hooray and up she rises".',
    // Sources: song facts [Cecil J. Sharp, English Folk-Chanteys (1914), note to No. 2: sung coming into port, the shantyman saying what the crew thought of the ship]; song facts [Sharp (1914), contents, introduction and note to No. 2: among the capstan shanties; this version the usual form]; credits: words and tune traditional (John Short's version, as noted by Sharp, 1914); licence.source: Sharp, English Folk-Chanteys (London and Taunton, 1914), No. II, first version, p. 3, note p. 65, in E dorian, verses 1 to 3 of seven, archive.org/details/englishfolkchant00shar; decisions.md row 2026-11-08 (the soprano the shantyman; the chorus on Sharp's harmony); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'leave-her-johnny' => 'Leave Her, Johnny is a homeward-bound shanty. Sailors usually sang it as the ship was coming into port, and the shantyman used it to say what the crew thought of the ship they were about to leave. Cecil Sharp put it among the capstan shanties, for steady work like marching round the capstan or working the pumps, and called this version the usual form of the tune. Our arrangement is read from his English Folk-Chanteys of 1914, as John Short of Watchet sang it, in E dorian as printed, on the harmony of Sharp\'s piano part. This is three verses for 2 to 4 singers, the soprano singing each grumble alone.',
    // Sources: song facts [Wikipedia, "Caro mio ben": credited to Giuseppe Giordani, as Parisotti prints it; probably by Tommaso Giordani, for a London concert around 1785; a favourite with voice students]; credits: words anonymous (18th century), music attributed to Giuseppe Giordani, probably Tommaso Giordani; licence.source: Anthology of Italian Song of the 17th and 18th Centuries, ed. Alessandro Parisotti (G. Schirmer, 1894), pp. 131 to 133, archive.org/details/anthologyofitali00pari; decisions.md rows 2026-11-08 (sung in Italian, the lower parts humming his piano harmony; the hummed parts breathe apart, Hillel); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'caro-mio-ben' => 'Caro mio ben is an Italian love song of the late 1700s, a favourite with voice students, who sing it to stretch their range and practise their technique. For centuries it was credited to Giuseppe Giordani, as Alessandro Parisotti\'s Anthology of Italian Song of 1894 prints it, but recent research suggests Tommaso Giordani probably wrote it, for a concert in London around 1785. Our arrangement is read from Parisotti\'s anthology, in E-flat: the soprano sings his voice line in Italian, and the alto, tenor and bass hum the harmony of his piano part on "oo". It is slow and tender, with time to breathe, for 1 to 4 singers.',
    // Sources: song facts [Wikipedia, "Hociel Thomas": she wrote the words with her father, became a blues singer and pianist, recorded in the mid-1920s]; song facts [Wikipedia, "George W. Thomas", and the 1924 printing: his own Chicago publishing company]; credits: words George W. Thomas and Hociel Thomas, music George W. Thomas (1924); licence.source: Shorty George Blues (Geo. W. Thomas Music Co., Chicago, 1924), stamped "MAR 14 1924", in F, written here a whole tone higher, five cleared verses; decisions.md rows 2026-11-08 (the lower parts hum his piano's chords; the hummed parts breathe apart, Hillel); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'shorty-george-blues' => 'Shorty George Blues is a slow twelve-bar blues of 1924, with music by George W. Thomas and words by him and his daughter, Hociel Thomas. George ran his own music publishing company in Chicago and printed this song himself. Hociel became a blues singer and pianist herself, and made records in the mid-1920s. Our arrangement is read from that 1924 printing, a whole tone higher, in five verses. The soprano sings the tune with its blue notes, and the alto, tenor and bass hum the chords of his piano part underneath, for 1 to 4 singers.',
    // Sources: song facts [Wikipedia, "O Holy Night": Cappeau's French words, December 1847, the stagecoach from Mâcon to Dijon, Midnight Mass in Roquemaure, Adam's setting within days; Dwight's English words of 1855, an abolitionist]; credits: words Placide Cappeau (1847), English John Sullivan Dwight (1855), music Adolphe Adam (1847); licence.source: Carols Old and Carols New, ed. Charles L. Hutchins (Boston: The Parish Choir, 1916), Carol 741, pp. 617 to 620, in E-flat, written here a minor third lower; decisions.md row 2026-11-08 (the refrain's choir parts, the verse on the organ's harmony); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'o-holy-night' => 'O Holy Night is Adolphe Adam\'s Christmas song. Placide Cappeau wrote the French words in December 1847, on a stagecoach from Mâcon to Dijon, for Midnight Mass in Roquemaure, and Adam set them within days. The English words are by John Sullivan Dwight, the American critic and minister, who wrote them in 1855; he was an abolitionist, and admired the song\'s stress on human equality. Our arrangement is read from Carols Old and Carols New, a Boston carol book of 1916, a minor third lower: its choir parts in the refrain, "Fall on your knees", and quiet chords on its organ harmony in the verse.',
    // Sources: [Wikipedia, "Deep River (song)": "an anonymous African-American spiritual, popularized by Henry Burleigh"; first mentioned in print in 1876, in the first edition of J. B. T. Marsh's The Story of the Jubilee Singers]; [Wikipedia, "Harry Burleigh": singer and composer]; licence.source: Burleigh's octavo (G. Schirmer, 8vo Choruses No. 5815, "Copyright, 1914, by G. Schirmer"), for mixed voices, in E-flat (an earlier 1913 printing is reported, so the text says what our copy is); licence.arrangement and decisions.md row 2026-11-10 (one line where the tenors or basses divide; Bass II's E-flat 2 an octave up in bars 8 and 27; 4 only); song file (the alto's tune twice, the soprano's entry at bar 9, the verse "Oh, don't you want to go")
    'deep-river' => 'Deep River is an anonymous African-American spiritual, first mentioned in print in 1876, in the first edition of J. B. T. Marsh\'s The Story of the Jubilee Singers, and popularised by the singer and composer Harry T. Burleigh. This is Burleigh\'s own setting for mixed chorus, as G. Schirmer printed it in 1914, in E-flat as he wrote it: where his tenors or basses divide we sing one line, and two low bass notes go up an octave. It is for 4 singers, slow and smooth: the alto sings the tune twice, the soprano joining the second time, on long notes above it, then everyone sings the verse, "Oh, don\'t you want to go".',
    // Sources: song facts [Hollis Dann Music Course, Fourth Year Music (American Book Company, 1916), p. 115, read from the scan: "May Bells", a round for two voices, II at bar 5]; song facts [Traditional Ballad Index, "White Coral Bells": earliest date 1916, the book names no author though it credits most of its other pieces]; credits: words and tune anonymous (printed 1916); licence.source: E-flat, written here a fourth lower in B-flat, up to four voices entering four bars apart; decisions.md row 2026-11-10 (both verses twice, the last note held to the bar line); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'white-coral-bells' => 'White Coral Bells is an American round about lilies of the valley. Its earliest known printing is "May Bells", in Hollis Dann\'s Fourth Year Music, a school music reader of 1916, which names no author for it, though it credits most of its other pieces. There it is a round for two voices. Our round is read from that 1916 page, with its words, "deck our garden walk" and "That could happen only when the fairies sing", a fourth lower, in B-flat. Here it is for 2 to 4 singers: everyone sings both verses twice, entering four bars apart, with the tenor and bass an octave lower, and its four lines sound together, one in each voice.',
    // Sources: song facts ["Daily Express" Community Song Book, ed. John Goss (London, 1927), p. 195, read from the scan: its four lines numbered for the voices' entries]; song facts [David Vinton, The Masonick Minstrel (Dedham, Massachusetts, 1816), p. 21, read from the scan: "Scotland's Burning! Canon." in four numbered lines, "Look out"]; credits: words and tune traditional; licence.source: the line ends numbered 4, 3, 2, 1, read as where each voice stops so all finish together, in G, written here a whole tone lower in F; Wikipedia, "John Goss (baritone)" (a baritone, editor of the 1927 book, died 1953); decisions.md row 2026-11-10 (three times through, everyone stopping together); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'londons-burning' => 'London\'s Burning is an English round about a fire and the call to put it out; a version called "Scotland\'s Burning" was printed in America in 1816. Our round is read from the Daily Express Community Song Book of 1927, edited by the baritone John Goss, a whole tone lower, in F. Goss numbers its four lines for the voices to come in, and numbers their ends 4, 3, 2, 1, which we read as where each voice stops, so that everyone finishes together. Here it is for 2 to 4 singers: the soprano sings it three times, each voice entering two bars after the last, the tenor and bass an octave lower, and all stop together on one chord.',
    // Sources: song facts [Notes and Queries, 7th series, vol. 2 (25 December 1886), p. 507, read from the scan: "the school round", the words in all the copies of the round he had met, "They all ran after the farmer's wife"]; song facts [Home Songs (Oliver Ditson Company, Boston, 1906), p. 95, read from the scan: a collection of favourite songs, hymns and rounds "for the fireside", "Three Blind Mice (round in four parts)"]; credits: words and tune traditional; licence.source: in D, written here a whole tone lower in C, entries four bars apart; decisions.md row 2026-11-10 (twice through, the 1906 words); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'three-blind-mice' => 'Three Blind Mice is the nursery round almost everyone knows. In 1886 a reader of Notes and Queries called it "the school round" and quoted the words in all the printed copies he had met, "They all ran after the farmer\'s wife". Our round is read from Home Songs, a Boston book of 1906 of songs, hymns and rounds, which prints it in four parts, with its words "She cut off their tails" and "Did ever you see such a sight", a whole tone lower, in C. Here it is for 2 to 4 singers: everyone sings it twice, entering four bars apart, with the tenor and bass an octave lower, and the parts tumble over each other.',
    // Sources: song facts [Wikipedia (German), "O wie wohl ist mir am Abend": a German round, usually credited to Karl Friedrich Schulz, oldest known printing in his singing method of 1812, authorship not certain]; song facts [H. F. Sefton, Three-Part Songs (Toronto, 1869), p. 41, read from the scan: an earlier English version, "When to rest the bells are ringing"]; credits: English words anonymous (printed 1896); licence.source: The Educational Music Course, First Reader (Ginn & Company, Boston, 1896), p. 22, "Round" in three parts six bars apart, in F, written here a whole tone lower in E-flat; decisions.md row 2026-11-10 (three times through); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'oh-how-lovely' => 'Oh, How Lovely Is the Evening is a round about evening bells. It comes from a German round, "O wie wohl ist mir am Abend", whose oldest known printing is in Karl Friedrich Schulz\'s singing method of 1812, though whether he wrote it is not certain. Our round is read from the First Reader of The Educational Music Course, a Boston school music book of 1896, which prints it as a round in three parts, a whole tone lower, in E-flat. Its third line is the bells themselves, "Ding, dong" on one low note. Here it is for 2 to 4 singers: everyone sings it three times, entering six bars apart, with the tenor and bass an octave lower.',
    // Sources: song facts [Dett, ed., Religious Folk-Songs of the Negro as Sung at Hampton Institute (1927), pp. 60 and 61, read from the scan: the refrain marked "Tutti", the first line of each verse "Solo quasi extempore sed meme ritmo", the second "Solo capriccioso"]; song facts [the same, p. 60: among the "Hymns on Biblical Themes", marked "Poco allegro ritmo marcato"]; credits: words and tune traditional spiritual, as sung at Hampton Institute (printed 1909), arrangement Stackapella; licence.arrangement: original, the printed four-part setting used only to check the chords; licence.source: Dett (1927), pp. 60 to 61, the same setting as the 1909 New Edition, in A-flat and written here a semitone down in G; the refrain, then verses 1 and 2 of the three printed, each followed by the refrain; description: the soprano sings each verse line alone, everyone answers "'Way in de middle ob de air"; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'ezekiel-saw-the-wheel' => 'Ezekiel Saw the Wheel is a spiritual as Hampton Institute sang it. Its 1927 songbook, edited by R. Nathaniel Dett, files it among the "Hymns on Biblical Themes", fairly lively, with a firm, marked rhythm. It marks the refrain "Tutti", for everyone, and the first line of each verse "Solo quasi extempore": for one voice, as if made up on the spot. Our tune and words are read from that book, a semitone lower, in G; the four parts are our own, checked against its chords. This is the refrain and two of its three verses for 2 to 4 singers: the soprano sings each verse\'s solo lines alone, and everyone answers "\'Way in de middle ob de air".',
    // Sources: song facts [Dett, ed., Religious Folk-Songs of the Negro as Sung at Hampton Institute (1927), introduction, section IX, p. xviii: tunes and harmonies set down as the students sang them, in a tradition going back to 1868]; credits: words and tune traditional spiritual, as sung at Hampton Institute (printed 1909), arrangement Stackapella; licence.arrangement: original, the printed four-part refrain used only to check the chords; licence.source: Dett (1927), p. 169, "Ev'ry Time I Feel the Spirit", the same setting as the 1909 New Edition, in E-flat as printed and as written here; the refrain twice as its repeat marks say, then verses 1 and 2 of the three printed, each followed by the refrain twice; the solo verse sung by the alto; description: at an easy walking pace; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'every-time-i-feel-the-spirit' => 'Every Time I Feel the Spirit is a spiritual as Hampton Institute sang it. R. Nathaniel Dett, who edited the school\'s songbook in 1927, wrote that its tunes and harmonies were set down as the students sang them, in a tradition going back to 1868. Our tune and words are read from that book, where it is called Ev\'ry Time I Feel the Spirit, in E-flat as printed; the four parts are our own, checked against its chords. This is the refrain, sung twice as its repeat marks say, and two of its three verses, each followed by the refrain, for 2 to 4 singers, at an easy walking pace: the alto sings each verse alone.',
    // Sources: song facts [Dett, ed., Religious Folk-Songs of the Negro as Sung at Hampton Institute (1927), p. 104, read from the scan: the related "Did You Hear How Dey Crucified My Lord?"; "Were You There" seemed "to be universally more popular"]; credits: words and tune traditional spiritual, as sung at Hampton Institute (published 1909), arrangement Stackapella; licence.arrangement: original, the printed four-part setting used only to check the chords; licence.source: Dett (1927), Appendix p. VI, reprinting the 1909 New Edition, p. 162, in G as printed and as written here, verses 1 to 4 of the 5; description: the soprano asks each question alone, the others echo it under her held note, slow and quiet; song file (the parts' guide notes: the echo is "were you there?" in verses 1 and 4, "to the tree?" and "in the side?" in verses 2 and 3)
    'were-you-there' => 'Were You There is a spiritual as Hampton Institute\'s singers sang it. The school\'s songbook, edited by R. Nathaniel Dett in 1927, also prints a related spiritual, "Did You Hear How Dey Crucified My Lord?", and notes that this one seemed "to be universally more popular". Our tune and words are read from that book\'s reprint of the 1909 setting, in G as printed; the four parts are our own, checked against its chords. This is four of its five verses for 2 to 4 singers, slow and quiet: the soprano asks each verse\'s question alone, the others echo its end under her held note, and everyone sings the rest together.',
    // Sources: song facts [Seward, Jubilee Songs (Biglow & Main, 1872), preface, p. 3, and pp. 16 and 17: more than half the Fisk melodies without the fourth and seventh; this tune uses only five notes]; credits: words and tune traditional spiritual, as sung by the Fisk Jubilee Singers (published 1872), arrangement Stackapella; licence.arrangement: original (the source prints the tune alone, "Sung in Unison"); licence.source: Seward (1872), pp. 16 to 17, in G minor, written here a tone up in A minor; the refrain, then verses 1, 2 and 4 of the 4, each into the refrain; description: the soprano sings each verse alone, everyone joins its last line, steady and rhythmic; song file (the parts' guide notes: the verses' last lines are "And why not every man?", "King Jesus shall be mine." and "And I'll never come back any more.")
    'didnt-my-lord-deliver-daniel' => 'Didn\'t My Lord Deliver Daniel is a spiritual from the Fisk Jubilee Singers\' book of 1872, Theodore F. Seward\'s Jubilee Songs. Seward noted that more than half the book\'s melodies leave out the fourth and seventh notes of the scale; this tune is one of them, using only five notes. Our tune and words are read from its unison melody, a tone higher, in A minor; the four parts are our own. This is the refrain and three of its four verses for 2 to 4 singers: the soprano sings each verse alone, and everyone joins its last line.',
    // Sources: song facts [Dett, ed., Religious Folk-Songs of the Negro as Sung at Hampton Institute (1927), p. 88, read from the scan: headed "Transcribed from the singing of the Hampton Institute students", among the "Hymns of Consolation"]; credits: words traditional spiritual, as sung by the students of Hampton Institute (printed 1927); tune as R. Nathaniel Dett transcribed it; arrangement R. Nathaniel Dett's own four parts (1927), adapted by Stackapella; licence.arrangement: Dett's four-part refrain, a whole tone lower, every pitch as printed but a few listed changes; licence.source: Dett (1927), p. 88, in B major, written here a whole tone lower in A; the refrain, verse 1, the refrain, verse 3 and the refrain (verse 2 left out for length); description: the soprano sings each verse alone, slow and very expressive; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'balm-in-gilead' => 'There Is a Balm in Gilead is a spiritual that R. Nathaniel Dett printed in 1927 in his songbook of Hampton Institute\'s spirituals, headed "Transcribed from the singing of the Hampton Institute students": the four parts were written down as the students sang them, and filed among the "Hymns of Consolation". Our arrangement is read from that page, Dett\'s own four parts, a whole tone lower, in A. It is for 2 to 4 singers, slow and very expressive: everyone sings the refrain, twice through each time, and between refrains the soprano sings a verse alone, "Sometimes I feel discouraged", then "If you cannot preach like Peter", the first and last of the three printed.',
    // Sources: song facts [Sharp, English Folk-Chanteys (1914), note to No. 35, p. 71, read from the scan: taken down from John Short of Watchet; no variants of it in his hands, and no printed version known to him]; song facts [Sharp (1914), p. 32 and pp. 40 and 41: a pulling chantey, the pulls marked with arrow-heads, two in each chorus, on "Way" and on the "bully" right after it]; credits: words and tune traditional (as sung by John Short, Watchet; printed 1914), arrangement Stackapella, on Cecil Sharp's harmony; licence.arrangement: on the harmony of Sharp's piano accompaniment; licence.source: Sharp (1914), No. XXXV, pp. 40 to 41, in G major as printed and as written here; the refrain and verse 1 as printed, verses 2 and 3 from the two lines Sharp prints after the tune; description: the soprano the shantyman, everyone answers "Way ay bully in the alley"; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'bully-in-the-alley' => 'Bully in the Alley is a pulling shanty that Cecil Sharp took down from John Short of Watchet. Sharp wrote that he had no other version of it and knew of no printed one. He marks with arrow-heads the notes the crew pulled on, two in each chorus, on "Way" and on the "bully" right after it. Our arrangement is read from his English Folk-Chanteys of 1914, in G as printed, on the harmony of his piano part, with verses 2 and 3 made from the two lines he prints after the tune. This is the refrain and three verses for 2 to 4 singers: the soprano is the shantyman, and everyone answers "Way ay bully in the alley".',
    // Sources: song facts [Sharp, English Folk-Chanteys (1914), introduction, p. xiii, and No. 37, pp. 42 and 43: the chanteyman often began by singing the refrain alone, to announce the chantey and remind the crew of the tune; this one starts that way]; song facts [Sharp (1914), introduction, p. xi: his singer more than fifty years in sailing ships, for most of them a recognised chanteyman]; credits: words and tune traditional (as sung by John Short, Watchet; printed 1914), arrangement Stackapella, on Cecil Sharp's harmony; licence.arrangement: on the harmony of Sharp's piano accompaniment; licence.source: Sharp (1914), No. XXXVII, pp. 42 to 43, in C major, written here a tone up in D; every word and section as printed (the solo introduction, two verses each with its chorus, and the full chorus); the pull on each chorus's last "haul"; description: the soprano the shantyman; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'haul-on-the-bowline' => 'Haul on the Bowline is a pulling shanty as John Short of Watchet sang it to Cecil Sharp, who wrote that his singer had spent more than fifty years in sailing ships. Sharp writes that the chanteyman often began by singing the refrain alone, to announce the chantey and remind the crew of the tune, and this one starts that way. Our arrangement is read from his English Folk-Chanteys of 1914, a tone higher, in D, on the harmony of his piano part. This is the call, two verses and the full chorus for 2 to 4 singers: the soprano is the shantyman, and everyone lands together on each answer\'s last "haul", where Sharp marks the pull.',
    // Sources: song facts [Sharp, Folk Songs from Somerset, Fifth Series (1909), note to No. 124, pp. 89 and 90, read from the scan: "a capstan chanty"; Captain Lewis of Minehead sang it as a song, not as a chanty; round the capstan it went much faster; Sharp set his harmony for the slower pace]; song facts [the same note: the old minor form; the modernized major version "far less beautiful"]; credits: words and tune traditional (as sung by Captain Lewis, Minehead; printed 1909), arrangement Stackapella, on Cecil Sharp's harmony; licence.arrangement: the soprano Sharp's voice part note for note, the other parts on the chords of his accompaniment, the last sung chord A major as in his piano postlude; licence.source: Sharp, Folk Songs from Somerset, Fifth Series (1909), No. CXXIV, pp. 62 to 64, in A minor at the printed pitch, verse 1 and the chorus; sections: Verse 1, Chorus, Chorus; song file (the parts' guide notes: the alto's C-sharp on the last "leagues.")
    'spanish-ladies' => 'Spanish Ladies is a capstan shanty, the sailors\' farewell to Spain, as Cecil Sharp took it down from Captain Lewis of Minehead and printed it in Folk Songs from Somerset in 1909. Captain Lewis sang it as a song, not as a chanty; round the capstan, Sharp notes, it went much faster, and he set his harmony for the slower pace. Sharp prints the tune in its old minor form, finding the major version sailors of his day sang "far less beautiful". Our arrangement is read from his book, in A minor, on the harmony of his piano part, ending on his postlude\'s major chord. This is the first verse and the chorus twice for 2 to 4 singers.',
    // Sources: song facts [Terry, The Shanty Book, Part I (1921), No. 15, pp. 32 and 33: a halliard shanty, for hoisting the sails; the shantyman sings each line alone and the crew answers "Blow, boys blow"]; song facts [Terry (1921), note to No. 15, p. xv: spotless decks and masts and yards that "shone like silver" the marks of a Yankee packet ship, kept that way by a terrible discipline]; credits: words and tune traditional (as printed by R. R. Terry, 1921), arrangement Stackapella, on R. R. Terry's harmony; licence.arrangement: the soprano Terry's voice part note for note, the other parts the chorus lines only, on his chords, a whole tone higher; licence.source: Terry (1921), No. 15, "Blow my bully boys", pp. 32 and 33, in F major, written here a whole tone higher in G, verses 1, 2, 5, 6, 7 and 8 of Terry's eight; description: a Yankee ship and what her crew got to eat; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'blow-boys-blow' => 'Blow, Boys, Blow is a halliard shanty, sung for hoisting the sails, about a Yankee ship and what her crew got to eat. Richard Terry notes that spotless decks, and masts and yards that "shone like silver", were the marks of a Yankee packet ship, kept that way by a terrible discipline. Our arrangement is read from his Shanty Book of 1921, where it is called Blow my bully boys, a whole tone higher, in G, on the harmony of his piano part. This is six of his eight verses for 2 to 4 singers: the soprano is the shantyman, singing each solo line alone, and everyone answers "Blow, boys blow." and "Blow my bully boys blow."',
    // Sources: song facts [The Jewish Encyclopedia, vol. 8 (1904), "Ma'oz Zur": the first letters of its stanzas spell the poet's name, Mordecai, who probably lived in the thirteenth century; first sung at home after lighting the Hanukkah lights, later in the synagogue too]; song facts [the same article: the tune adapted from an old German folk song, "So weiss ich eins, dass mich erfreut", widespread among German Jews by about 1450]; credits: words a Hebrew piyyut signed Mordecai in its acrostic (13th century), tune traditional Ashkenazi, arrangement Stackapella on A. Z. Idelsohn's harmony (1912); licence.arrangement: on the harmony of Idelsohn's two and three printed voices; licence.source: Idelsohn, Sefer ha-Shirim (Berlin, 1912), No. 83, p. 77, in E major, kept in E major; verses 1 and 5, each with the printed repeat of its last line; transliterated in modern Israeli pronunciation; description: verse 1 praises God, the rock of my salvation, verse 5 tells the Hanukkah story of the oil; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'maoz-tzur' => 'Maoz Tzur is the Hanukkah hymn sung after lighting the Hanukkah lights, at first only at home and later in the synagogue too. The first letters of its stanzas spell the poet\'s name, Mordecai, and Eduard Birnbaum identified its tune as an adaptation of an old German folk song, "So weiss ich eins, dass mich erfreut", widespread among German Jews by about 1450. Our arrangement is read from A. Z. Idelsohn\'s Sefer ha-Shirim of 1912, in E major, on the harmony of his two and three voices. It is sung in Hebrew, transliterated, for 2 to 4 singers: verse 1, praising God, the Rock of my salvation, and verse 5, the Hanukkah story of the oil.',
    // Sources: song facts [Kisselgof, Lider-zamelbukh far der yidisher shul un familye, No. 24, p. 32, read from the scan: his Yiddish songbook "for the Jewish school and family", first published in St Petersburg in 1911 and 1912, the tune marked "Chsidisch", a Hasidic melody]; song facts [the same book, words p. 13: Mordkhe Rivesman's words paint the holiday at home: dreidels every night, sizzling-hot latkes, lighting the thin little candles and saying "Al hanisim", for the miracles]; credits: words Mordkhe Rivesman, tune traditional Hasidic, arrangement Stackapella on Pesach Lvov's arrangement (1911/12), revised for the second edition (1914) under A. Zhitomirsky (died 1937); Lvov died 22 February 1913 [the book's own memorial page, "tsum ondenk fun P. Lvov", 4th ed. p. 6, signed Z. Kisselgof, January 1914; the Jewish Encyclopedia of Russia (1995): Lvov, Pavel (Pesakh) Ruvimovich, 1880 Kiev to 1913 Nervi]; licence.arrangement: the soprano and alto Lvov's two voices as printed, the tenor and bass from his piano's left hand; licence.source: read from the fourth edition (Berlin), in F minor, kept in F minor; the Yiddish transliterated in YIVO-style romanization; description: verse 2 praises Judah the Maccabee, the bass on "dm"; sections: Verse 1, Verse 2, Verse 1 again; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'oy-chanukah' => 'Oy Chanukah is a Yiddish Hanukkah song that Susman Kisselgof printed in his songbook "for the Jewish school and family", first published in St Petersburg in 1911 and 1912, marking its tune "Chsidisch": a Hasidic melody. Mordkhe Rivesman\'s words paint the holiday at home: dreidels every night, sizzling-hot latkes, lighting the thin little candles and saying "Al hanisim", for the miracles. Our arrangement is read from the book\'s fourth edition, in F minor as printed: the soprano and alto sing Pesach Lvov\'s two voices, and the tenor and bass take his piano\'s left hand. It is sung in Yiddish, transliterated, for 2 to 4 singers: verse 1, verse 2, praising Judah the Maccabee, then verse 1 again.',
    // Sources: song facts [The Union Hymnal (CCAR, 1897), No. 17, pp. 140 and 141, read from the scan: the hymnal of the Central Conference of American Rabbis prints it in four parts among its "Hebrew Hymns and Responses", with all five verses]; song facts [The Union Hymnal (1932), No. 292, and (1897), index p. 218: the 1932 edition credits it to Julius Freudenthal; the 1897 edition\'s index gives the music\'s source only as "German"]; credits: words traditional Hebrew liturgy, tune Julius Freudenthal (1843), arrangement the Union Hymnal's four parts (1897), adapted by Stackapella; licence.arrangement: adapted for 2 to 4 beginners; licence.source: the Union Hymnal (1897), in F, written here a tone up in G; verses 1 to 5 sung in order; the words respelled in modern Israeli pronunciation; description: a Hebrew hymn of the Sabbath morning service; its five short verses praise God as our God, our Lord, our King and our Saviour; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'ein-keloheinu' => 'Ein Keloheinu is a Hebrew hymn of the Sabbath morning service. The Union Hymnal of 1897 prints it in four parts among its "Hebrew Hymns and Responses", its index giving the music\'s source only as "German"; the 1932 edition credits it to Julius Freudenthal. Our arrangement is read from the 1897 book, a tone higher than its F, in G. All five short verses are sung in Hebrew, transliterated, for 2 to 4 singers: there is none like our God, our Lord, our King, our Saviour; who is like our God; we give thanks; blessed is our God; you are our God. It is sung with the substituted names customary when singing outside prayer: Elokeinu and Kelokeinu.',
    // Sources: song facts [The Jewish Encyclopedia, vol. 1 (1901), "Addir Hu": the tune first found in a Haggadah in Hebrew, Latin and German that J. S. Rittangel, a professor at Königsberg, published in 1644, already set with a bass part]; credits: words traditional Hebrew hymn of the Passover Haggadah, tune traditional, arrangement Stackapella; licence.arrangement: original, written for Stackapella; licence.source: The Union Haggadah, revised edition (CCAR, 1923), p. 81, in C, voice and piano, written here in C as printed, the piano part used only to check the chords; three verses; the words respelled in modern Israeli pronunciation; description: Adir Hu ("Mighty is He"), the Passover Seder song; the three verses the Union Haggadah prints, of the hymn's eight; each asks God to build His house soon, speedily, in our days; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'adir-hu' => 'Adir Hu, "Mighty is He", is a song of the Passover Seder. The earliest known printing of its tune, in an early form, is in a Haggadah in Hebrew, Latin and German that J. S. Rittangel published in 1644. Our tune and words are read from the Union Haggadah of 1923, in C as printed, and the four parts are our own. It is sung in Hebrew, transliterated, for 2 to 4 singers: the book\'s three short verses, which take their names for God from across the hymn\'s eight stanzas, each praising God and asking Him to build His house soon, in our days. It is sung with the substituted name customary when singing outside prayer: Kel, for El.',
    // Sources: song facts [A. Z. Idelsohn, Jewish Music in its Historical Development (copyright 1929; read from the scan of the 1944 Tudor printing), p. 401, Table XXXII, No. 1: printed in his history of Jewish music among the "Tunes of the Eastern Ashkenazic folk-song"]; Idelsohn (1929), pp. 360 to 361, the archive.org text of the 1944 printing: songs of Elijah sung at the outgoing of Sabbath already in the eleventh century, this refrain the oldest part; song facts [the same page, the words under the tune: they call on the prophet Elijah by three names, Elijah the prophet, Elijah the Tishbite, Elijah of Gilead]; credits: words traditional Hebrew refrain, tune traditional Eastern Ashkenazi folk tune (as printed by A. Z. Idelsohn, 1929), arrangement Stackapella; licence.arrangement: original, written for Stackapella; licence.source: Idelsohn (1929), p. 401, in E minor, the tune alone, no harmony; his text on p. 392 calls it one of two very popular tunes for 'Eliyohu Hannovi'; written here in G minor, a minor third up; only the metrical first section, up to the print's Fine, sung three times; respelled in modern Israeli pronunciation; description: the old Hebrew refrain that, as Idelsohn wrote, Jews have sung at the close of the Sabbath since the Middle Ages; sung three times through, the third time softly; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'eliyahu-hanavi' => 'Eliyahu HaNavi, "Elijah the Prophet", is an old Hebrew refrain sung at the close of the Sabbath, which A. Z. Idelsohn called the oldest part of the Elijah songs that Jewish communities already sang there in the eleventh century. It calls on the prophet by three names: the prophet, the Tishbite, the Gileadite. Our tune and words come from a 1944 printing of Idelsohn\'s 1929 history of Jewish music, among its "Tunes of the Eastern Ashkenazic folk-song". It is a minor third above his E minor, in G minor, with four parts of our own. Sung in Hebrew, transliterated, for 2 to 4 singers: the refrain\'s first part, those three names, three times through, the third time softly.',
    // Sources: song facts [The Union Haggadah (CCAR, 1923), pp. 86 to 92, read from the scan: it counts from one to thirteen, and each stanza repeats every number back down to one: one is our God, in heaven and on earth]; song facts [The Union Haggadah (1923), pp. 87 to 89: it changes two of the numbers, in its Hebrew as well as its English: eight becomes the eight days of Hanukkah, and nine the nine festivals]; credits: words traditional Hebrew counting song of the Passover Haggadah, tune traditional, arrangement Stackapella; licence.words: part of the Haggadah since about the fifteenth century, as the Union Haggadah's notes say; licence.tune: printed without a composer in the Union Haggadah (1923); licence.arrangement: original, written for Stackapella; licence.source: The Union Haggadah, revised edition (1923), p. 93, in D minor, voice and piano, written here in F minor, a minor third up, the piano part used only to check the chords; stanzas 1 to 4 sung; respelled in modern Israeli pronunciation; description: the counting song sung near the end of the Passover Seder; four mothers, three fathers, the two tablets of the covenant, and our one God in heaven and on earth; sung here from one to four; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'echad-mi-yodea' => 'Echad Mi Yodea, "Who Knows One?", is the counting song sung at the Passover Seder, in the Haggadah since about the fifteenth century, as the Union Haggadah\'s notes say. It counts from one to thirteen, each stanza counting back down to one. Our tune and words are read from the Union Haggadah of 1923, which prints the tune in D minor; here it is a minor third higher, in F minor, with four parts of our own. It is sung in Hebrew, transliterated, for 2 to 4 singers: verses one to four, from one is our God, in heaven and on earth, to the four mothers. It is sung with the substituted name customary when singing outside prayer: Elokeinu.',
    // Sources: song facts [The Union Haggadah (CCAR, 1923), pp. 84 and 85, read from the scan: printed as a solo verse and a chorus; one singer leads each verse, and everyone answers "Lecha ulecha", yours and yours]; song facts [The Union Haggadah (CCAR, 1908), pp. 54 and 55, read from the scan: already in the book's first edition of 1908, the same tune in the same key, with only its first verse]; credits: words traditional Hebrew hymn of the Passover Haggadah, tune traditional, arrangement Stackapella; licence.words: added to the Haggadah in the fifteenth century, as the Union Haggadah's notes say; licence.tune: printed as 'Traditional' in the Union Haggadah (1908 and 1923); licence.arrangement: original, written for Stackapella; licence.source: The Union Haggadah, revised edition (1923), pp. 84 to 85, in C, voice and piano, written here in D, a tone up, the piano part used only to check the chords; the three verses it prints sung here; the soprano sings the solo verses alone and everyone sings the chorus, as printed; respelled in modern Israeli pronunciation; description: Ki Lo Na'eh ("For to Him it is fitting"), the Passover Seder hymn; each verse praises God as mighty, holy and strong in His kingship; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'ki-lo-naeh' => 'Ki Lo Na\'eh, "For to Him it is fitting", is a Passover Seder hymn. The Union Haggadah, whose notes say it was added to the Haggadah in the fifteenth century, prints it as a solo verse and chorus: one singer leads each verse, and everyone answers "Lecha ulecha", yours and yours. Our tune and words are read from the 1923 edition, a tone higher than its C, in D, with four parts of our own. It is sung in Hebrew, transliterated, for 2 to 4 singers: three verses praising God as mighty, holy and strong in His kingship. It is sung with the substituted name customary when singing outside prayer: Adoshem.',
];
